// CS111 lab policy: Sublime must NOT run Python files. Ctrl+B just opens a
// terminal at the home folder; students compile and run by hand from there.
//
// WHY: when Ctrl+B runs the file for them, students never notice WHERE the file
// lives — and then submit the wrong file to the grader. Making them pwd/ls/cd to
// their own file is the point of the exercise, not an obstacle to it.
//
// HOW THIS WORKS: Sublime resolves package resources by path, and a loose file on
// disk WINS over the same path inside a .sublime-package zip. This file therefore
// shadows the "Python.sublime-build" that ships inside Sublime's own
// Python.sublime-package, whose build is ["python3", "-u", "$file"]. That built-in
// never loads, so Ctrl+B, F7, Ctrl+Shift+B and Tools > Build all reach THIS file
// instead. Because the BUILD is neutered rather than the keys, no keymap changes
// are needed and the Tools menu is covered for free.
//
// The override is per-FILE, not per-package: Python syntax highlighting,
// completions and the rest of Python.sublime-package are unaffected.
//
// The built-in "Syntax Check" variant (python3 -m py_compile) is deliberately not
// redefined here, so it disappears along with the run build.
//
// DELIBERATE: opens at HOME (cd ~), NOT at the file's directory, and says NOTHING
// — no path, no filename, no instructions. Pre-navigating or hinting would defeat
// the point. Neither $file_path nor $file_name is referenced anywhere. The lone
// echo below fires only when no terminal exists at all, so Ctrl+B never looks dead.
//
// `cd ~` uses a tilde, not $HOME, on purpose: Sublime expands $-variables itself
// before the shell sees this string, so a shell variable could be mangled. Tilde
// is expanded by the shell and Sublime leaves it alone. For the same reason no
// shell variables are used in the terminal-detection chain below.
//
// Each Ctrl+B opens a NEW terminal window; repeated presses open several.
//
// To re-enable the normal build, delete this file. Nothing else references it.
{
	"selector": "source.python",
	"shell_command": "cd ~ 2>/dev/null; if command -v x-terminal-emulator >/dev/null 2>&1; then nohup x-terminal-emulator >/dev/null 2>&1 & elif command -v gnome-terminal >/dev/null 2>&1; then nohup gnome-terminal >/dev/null 2>&1 & elif command -v konsole >/dev/null 2>&1; then nohup konsole >/dev/null 2>&1 & elif command -v xfce4-terminal >/dev/null 2>&1; then nohup xfce4-terminal >/dev/null 2>&1 & elif command -v xterm >/dev/null 2>&1; then nohup xterm >/dev/null 2>&1 & else echo 'No terminal emulator found - press Alt+` for the built-in terminal.'; fi"
}
